# Line endings are decided here and not by each clone's configuration. Two
# toolchains across three operating systems is the setting in which a diff fills
# with line ending noise, and a contributor whose clone happens to have
# core.autocrlf set one way produces a diff nobody else can read.
#
# eol=lf rather than eol=native, in the index and in the working tree both. A
# working tree that differs from the index by line ending is where the confusion
# starts: a tool that reads a file from disk and one that reads it from git see
# different bytes, and neither of them is wrong.
#
# What the tree holds is printed rather than restated here:
#
#     git ls-files | sed 's|.*/||' | sed -n 's/.*\(\.[A-Za-z0-9]*\)$/\1/p' | sort -u
#
# and how git is reading each tracked file after these rules apply:
#
#     git ls-files --eol

# The default. text=auto leaves git to detect a binary file and to leave it
# alone, so a future image or font is not normalised into corruption by this
# line. Everything git reads as text is LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The text types in the tree today, declared rather than detected. text is not
# text=auto here, because detection reading one of these as binary would silence
# the rule for that file without saying so.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf
*.rs   text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf

# The tracked files with no extension, anchored to the root so a later file of
# the same name somewhere else does not inherit a rule aimed at these. /build is
# a shell script, where a carriage return is not cosmetic: it reaches the
# interpreter as part of the last word on the line.
/DCO     text eol=lf
/LICENSE text eol=lf
/build   text eol=lf
/.nvmrc  text eol=lf
